---
// src/pages/bucknell/index.astro  →  arizvi.com/bucknell
// For the Bucknell business analytics search (Analytics & Operations Management, Freeman College of Management).
// Rebuilt on 13 Nov 2026 the way /tulane was (Ali: "Let's build this like we built Tulane and only keep the data
// visualization course and put it into a shell that feels like moodle"). In order: the 4 steps across the page under
// number tags, carrying his methods, with a line on generative AI under them; then one course in full (a section of
// ANOP 270 on its current outline, taught in Tableau), laid out the way it would sit in Moodle (MoodleShell.astro,
// content in src/data/bucknell270.ts); then ANOP 330, machine learning, as a card that opens its own page (the other
// course pages came off that day, and this one came back the same morning: "can you put the ML course back?").
// Bucknell's orange (the section colour) marks where Ali comes in: the step tags, where you are in the course shell,
// what the sample student has done, and a rubric's best level.
// Numbers up to two digits are words in the copy; digits stay for course codes, data, the week labels and points in
// the course shell, and the scores (1 or 0).
// Not linked from the nav; sends noindex.
// Bucknell facts, read 13 Nov 2026: ANOP 270's title, credit, description and prerequisites from the 2026-27
// catalogue's ANOP course listing (coursecatalog.bucknell.edu/courses/anop/); ANOP 270 or HUMN 270 in the Business
// Analytics Major Requirements from the major's catalogue page.
// The clients and the guest judge are not named: they are placeholders.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import MoodleShell from '../../components/MoodleShell.astro';
import { bucknellColours, course } from '../../data/bucknell';

// The 4 steps Ali teaches by, each carrying the methods he describes for them (as on /tulane).
const steps = [
  ['Build the course backward from the outcome.', 'I start from what students should be able to do when the course ends, then plan each week and each assessment backward from it. Every brief follows TILT (its purpose, the task, and the criteria), and I grade key pieces with specifications: each spec is met or not yet, 1 or 0.'],
  ['Personalize within a defined structure.', 'Everyone gets the same foundations. We start on a worked example and move to real problems with feedback midway, and by the end students solve semi-structured problems alone or in teams. I scaffold each step so that first-generation students and students whose first language is not English do well.'],
  ['Learn on real problems.', 'The problems come from open data such as the CFPB\'s consumer complaints, and from industry partners and my own industry work. Guest speakers bring more of them into the room. I prepare each speaker beforehand, and when students lose the thread, I step in with questions.'],
  ['Test the teaching itself.', 'The real problems midway are formative assessments. They show whether the teaching has worked while there is still time to change it. For mid-course feedback, students get fifteen minutes in class to answer three questions anonymously (what should I start, continue, and stop doing?), and I leave the room. At Bucknell I would run it in Moodle.'],
];

// ANOP 330, on its own page, with the 2 classes to try there.
const ml = course('anop-330');
const classes = [
  { week: 'Week 4', href: '/bucknell/anop-330/#overfit', title: 'Testing a model on days it has not seen', line: 'Students fit ice-cream sales on 16 days and test the model on the 8 held back.' },
  { week: 'Week 8', href: '/bucknell/anop-330/#threshold', title: 'Choosing the cutoff for a retention offer', line: 'Students find that the most accurate cutoff makes no money.' },
];
---
<Base title="Bucknell" description="How Ali Rizvi would teach in Bucknell's Analytics & Operations Management department, with one section of ANOP 270 (data visualization, in Tableau) laid out in full, and ANOP 330 (machine learning)." noindex accent={bucknellColours} rail>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <div class="text">
        <h1>Teaching analytics at <mark>Bucknell</mark></h1>
        <p>This page is a sample of how I could teach. I would work with the professors who teach these courses now to understand the students and see what works and what does not. Then I would build on what is already there.</p>
        <p>I spent over a decade in industry before my PhD, including leading AI and analytics teams at Morgan Stanley and on Amazon Alexa.</p>
        <p>We talked about the department needing more sections of data visualization, so the course I lay out in full is one section of ANOP&nbsp;270, taught in Tableau.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="photo"><img src="/media/headshot.jpg" alt="Ali Rizvi" width="400" height="500" /></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <Section title="How I teach" id="approach">
    <ol class="principles steps across">
      {steps.map(([head, line]) => <li><strong>{head}</strong> {line}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <div class="grid after-steps"><div class="span-8">
      <p><strong>Generative AI.</strong> Where students will use it at work, they use it in my courses too, and they disclose which tools and prompts they used and write a short reflection on what changed. Specific parts of that disclosure are graded 1 or 0. Where students need the fundamentals without it, the checks are on paper. I have also built generative AI modules into other courses, so that students learn to use it within each subject.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="A section of ANOP 270, in Tableau" id="course">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Here is a draft of how I could run one section of Data Visualization for Business Analytics on its current outline. I would settle the grading with the professors who teach it now, so that sections stay comparable, and check with whoever teaches HUMN&nbsp;270, since majors can take either. The content follows the catalog: the principles of data visualization and data preparation, and software for presenting data in tables, charts, and dashboards. Students work in Tableau Desktop and Tableau Prep, and end with two dashboards for a client who has a decision to make.</p>
      <p>The course starts on a worked example, a flower farm that has to give up one market day a week, and <a href="#mdl-worked">you can try it</a> in the first section. Midway, it runs as a case competition on real data, judged by a guest. Every team gets the same question, and I coach each team on how to prepare and how to present. By the end, each team builds for a client of its own.</p>
      <p>Below is the section as it might look in Moodle.</p>
    </div></div>
    <MoodleShell />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Machine learning: ANOP 330" id="ml">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>I could also teach a section of ANOP&nbsp;330. It has its own page, with two classes you can try and a full pack (syllabus, weekly plan, and rubrics) for colleagues to review.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ul class="exhibits courses two">
      <li class="course">
        <p class="code">{ml.code}</p>
        <h3><a href={`/bucknell/${ml.slug}/`}>{ml.title}</a></h3>
        <p>{ml.line}</p>
        <p>At Amazon I owned Alexa Orchestration, and at Morgan Stanley my team deployed AI models to compliance users. I would teach from both.</p>
        <dl class="course-facts">
          <dt class="label">In the catalog</dt>
          <dd>1 Credit. {ml.offered}. Prerequisites: {ml.prereqs}.</dd>
          <dt class="label">Students</dt>
          <dd>{ml.who}</dd>
          <dt class="label">When</dt>
          <dd>{ml.when}</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="sample"><a href={`/bucknell/${ml.slug}/`}>The term, week by week&nbsp;→</a></p>
      </li>
      <li class="course">
        <p class="label">Two classes to try</p>
        {classes.map((k) => (
          <div class="ml-class">
            <span class="label tile-tag">{ml.code} · {k.week}</span>
            <a class="tile-title" href={k.href}>{k.title}&nbsp;→</a>
            <p>{k.line}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

</Base>

<style>
  /* The 2 classes beside the ANOP 330 card. */
  .ml-class { margin-top: 18px; }
  .ml-class p { margin: 0; }
</style>
